From bd0f08bd142fca24a11c1f6c08a4c8fc67bbd91c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anurag Reddy Gunreddy <gunreddyg@google.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 07:29:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 7/7] feat(cluster): add MaxRefreshDelay

A lazy topology refresh, the one that MOVED, CLUSTERDOWN, TRYAGAIN,
LOADING, connection errors, closed connections and ShardsRefreshInterval
start, first waits a random delay of up to 1s, growing to 30s in
clusters of 1000 nodes. Until it finishes, commands are sent according
to the old topology: after a failover, retries keep going to the old
primary. MaxRefreshDelay sets the bound of that delay. The default, zero,
keeps the bound that scales with the number of nodes, and NewClient
rejects negative values (ErrInvalidMaxRefreshDelay).

The delay also limits how often a client refreshes while errors
continue, since no other lazy refresh starts while one waits, so a
shorter delay should come with MinRefreshInterval. On a local Valkey
9.0.6 cluster behind a discovery endpoint, with 200 clients, a 50ms
delay alone stormed in all 6 primary crashes, with 23,707-54,200
requests failing with ErrNoSlot; with MinRefreshInterval 250ms and
MaxSlotWait 5s as well, no run stormed and no request failed with
ErrNoSlot.

The README shows the three options together, and the failover scenario
and redirect loop tests also run with a 10ms delay.

Signed-off-by: Anurag Reddy Gunreddy <gunreddyg@google.com>
Co-authored-by: Vardhaman Sanjeev Viroje <viroje@google.com>
