+After a failover, commands keep going to the old primary until the client refreshes the topology, which it does
+after a random delay of up to 1s (more in clusters of 100 nodes or more). And while a shard has no primary, a
+request for one of its slots refreshes the topology and fails with `ErrNoSlot` at once, so a busy client can
+refresh the topology hundreds of times a second until the new primary appears. To follow failovers sooner
+without that storm, shorten the delay, bound the refreshes and let requests wait for the new primary:
 
